        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "feat(plugin-sdk): expose anonymous Talk activity to plugins",
        "body": "## Problem\n\nPlugins that render ambient voice UI, such as an avatar or speaking indicator, need to know when Talk is active. They should not need access to conversation audio, transcripts, provider details, or session identity.\n\n## Change\n\nAdds a narrow Plugin SDK observer:\n\n```ts\nconst stop = api.runtime.talk.watchActivity((event) => {\n  if (event.type === \"speech\") avatar.pulse();\n});\n```\n\nThe event stream contains only:\n\n- an opaque activity ID;\n- lifecycle events (`started` and `ended`);\n- conversational state (`listening`, `thinking`, `speaking`, and `error`);\n- speech pulses suitable for animation timing.\n\nIt never contains PCM, transcripts, session IDs, session keys, provider names, or credentials.\n\n## Architecture and performance\n\nActivity is recorded by the shared Talk session controller, so observers see canonical Talk activity regardless of which client or plugin started the conversation.\n\nWhen no plugin is watching, the hot path performs one subscriber-count check and returns. It allocates no activity ID, queues no events, inspects no media, and sends nothing. Watchers use independent bounded asynchronous queues, and a failing listener cannot interrupt Talk delivery.\n\nExact audio and microphone interaction are intentionally excluded from this API. The owner-scoped interactive API is proposed separately in #112528.\n\n## Companion implementation\n\nThe avatar plugin uses this feed to animate for Talk sessions started anywhere in OpenClaw:\nhttps://github.com/RomneyDa/openclaw-avatar-plugin\n\n## Validation\n\n- `pnpm build`\n- `pnpm check`\n- Talk activity and Plugin SDK runtime tests\n- Current-base testbox run cleared the plugin/tooling incompatibility from the previous branch base; remaining failures were unrelated existing browser-responsive and secrets-preset fixtures.",
